<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamikaze</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamikaze, meaning divine wind, is the Japanese name for the typhoons that destroyed the Mongol invasion fleets sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> in the late thirteenth century. The storms helped preserve Japanese independence. The term was later used for Japanese suicide pilots in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Japanese word kamikaze is usually translated as divine wind, from kami, meaning god, spirit or divinity, and kaze, wind. It originated as a makurakotoba, a fixed epithet of waka poetry, modifying the place name Ise. Since August 1281 it has been used for the major typhoons that dispersed the Mongol-Koryo fleets that invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> in 1274 and 1281.</p><p>The same characters can also be read shinpū, the Chinese-derived pronunciation. In 1937 a Japanese monoplane named Kamikaze made a record-breaking flight from Tokyo to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for the Asahi newspaper group; it was a prototype of the Mitsubishi Ki-15.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1944 and 1945 the word was attached to the Japanese special attack units, tokubetsu kōgekitai, whose aviators flew suicide attacks against Allied warships. The navy's air units were officially called shinpū tokubetsu kōgeki tai, divine wind special attack units. During the war itself the pronunciation kamikaze was used only informally in the Japanese press for these attacks; after the war it gained acceptance worldwide and was brought back into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>The attacks began in October 1944, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had lost several decisive battles and many of its best pilots, and no longer commanded the air or sea. The four subunits of the first Special Attack Force, Shikishima, Yamato, Asahi and Yamazakura, took their names from a poem by the classical scholar Motoori Norinaga on the Yamato spirit and mountain cherry blossom in the morning sun. Attacks reached a peak between April and June 1945 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okinawa">Battle of Okinawa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>About 3,800 kamikaze pilots died in the war, in attacks that killed more than 7,000 Allied naval personnel and sank several dozen warships; about 19 percent of attacks succeeded. Yet the attacks did not turn the tide: none sank a fleet carrier, cruiser or battleship at Okinawa, and the larger Allied fleet could send damaged ships home for repair. The pilots' training was harsh, with daily beatings recalled by survivors such as Daikichi Irokawa.</p><p>The pilots acted within a military culture that prized death over defeat or capture, and a schooling that since the Imperial Rescript on Education of 1890 had taught young Japanese that giving their lives for the state was the highest offering. Many hoped to be enshrined at Yasukuni as guardian spirits. Japanese cinema has returned to the subject repeatedly, from Saigo no Tokkōtai in 1970 to The Eternal Zero in 2013, and the word is now used generically for any attacking vehicle destroyed in its own attack.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kamikaze” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The storm was credited as the &quot;divine wind&quot;, <b>kamikaze</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Japanese expeditions were wrecked by storms, remembered in Japan as <b>kamikaze</b> or 'divine winds'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The song became popular in the armed forces, especially the Imperial Japanese Navy, and was widely sung during the Second World War, often by <b>kamikaze</b> pilots before take-off.</blockquote><a href="/songs/umi-yukaba">Umi Yukaba</a></li></ul></section>
